hi I'm Erik Bosse and this is a review  of Jurassic world fallen Kingdom the  follow up to 2015's Jurassic world in  the fifth movie in the universals  Jurassic Park franchise these Reese's  and installments are still super popular  but they tend to produce some mixed  feelings and viewers so here I'm gonna  break down what I think is the deeper  reason why and by the way this is a  spoiler free review I'm not gonna spoil  anything that hasn't already been pretty  much revealed in the trailers and promo  footage for the movie okay let's dive in  Jurassic world fallen Kingdom improves  on its predecessor in a number of ways  but he may still walk out of the movie  missing out on the charm of Steven  Spielberg's classic as many of us did  after Jurassic world in 2015 what the  1993 original had going for it was  simplicity within a preachy complex  sci-fi premise the whole idea came from  Michael Crichton's novel and by the way  this is the same author who gave us  Westworld so you can imagine how deep  Crichton's technology themes can push  your mind if you really want to go that  far with him but the Jurassic Park movie  kept it simple the Dino DNA cartoon Jeff  Goldblum's endlessly quotable rants and  an easy-to-follow family rescue  narrative some critics dismiss Jurassic  Park when it first came out but this  straightforwardness along with some  still impressive practical effects has  sustained the movie's appeal over the  years and many felt this was missing  from Jurassic world in 2015 the family  dynamic centered around characters whose  behavior came off as confusing now it  was arguably impressive how the movie  worked as a sort of self commentary on  the way capitalist Hollywood ruins a  good thing by over selling it but that  deeper message just got lost in the  movies logic issues and that brings us  to this sequel which is written by the  same team behind drastic world but now  directed by ji Bayona known for the  horror film the orphanage and the  disaster movie The Impossible both great  movies critics are saying fallen Kingdom  struggles with the same kind of logic  issues at Jurassic world did but the way  I see it fallen Kingdom benefits from an  improved premise and sequences that  embrace the spectacle and joy of the  original Jurassic Park it just seems to  get bored with the tone premise fallen  Kingdom picks up three years after  Jurassic world with Isla Nublar and the  ruins of the parks set to be destroyed  by a volcano wiping out all of the  dinosaurs as Congress debates whether to  intervene and save the dinos Claire  Bryce Dallas Howard  now a dinosaur conservation activist  takes an assignment from Benjamin  Lockwood former partner to John Hammond  to rescue some of the dinosaurs that  includes the Raptor blue requiring the  help of Owen Chris Pratt now this solid  premise creates three big benefits for  fallen kingdom one it's set up some  cleverly directed action  phaona is truly a gifted director when  it comes to visual spectacle and he  understands how to frame the dinosaurs  and interesting ways to deliver payoff  in the first half without spoiling  exactly what happens the movie opens  with a truly thrilling sequence set in a  rainstorm actually reminded me a lot of  the rainstorm sequence in the original  dress apart later there's a great cliff  side dive in a gyrus fear that recalls  the claustrophobia of Spielberg's  trailer sequence in the lost world and  Bayona shoots this in a compelling long  take the heroes narrowly escaped from  intricately plotted dilemmas and it's  all just very fun and this overall sense  of joy is what I assume people who are  tweeting about how much they enjoyed  this sequel are referring to the island  portion of the movie also leads to  another benefit for fallen Kingdom  humanizing the monsters by recasting  them as good guys I think one thing we  forget about the original Jurassic Park  was how much it avoided pure  demonization those dinos were natural  threats and human genetic engineers  meddling with that nature with the true  source of the evil in the movie  Spielberg included a lot of sweet  non-threatening moments the  Brachiosaurus sneezing on Lexi grins  pressing his ear against the sick  Triceratops inspiring the best and they  made a gift of all time at the end of  the movie the most heroic character is a  t-rex himself he fends off the raptors  and allows the humans to escape of  course Jurassic world tried to reproduce  this magic gave Owen a parental  connection with blue and it climax with  a showdown between the indominus rex and  the original t-rex unfortunately we just  didn't feel that same emotional  connection with those dinosaurs that we  did before and the decision to create  them with mostly CGI as opposed to  practical effects and prosthetic models  might have been a big part of that  but fallen Kingdom I would argue pulls  off those poignant moments with the  dinosaurs on Isla Nublar you really feel  their life-or-death crisis and the  sequel nearly pulls off a twist from one  of the best sequels of all time t2  Judgment Day  recasting the monster from the first  movie as a hero friendly to the  that makes you a little sad to lose now  to be fair teeth to also replace the  original villain with an even cooler  t1000 and these new dinos t1 thousands  but by letting this empathize with the  dinosaurs fallen kingdom's premise  restores the franchise's straightforward  rescue narrative as I said before the  original plot structure of Jurassic Park  isn't that complicated save the kids get  the hell out every Jurassic movie has  followed in the footsteps by including  children in the threat somehow to their  hang results honestly the success of a  Jurassic Park movie is directly  proportional to how much you care  whether or not the kids die but at the  end of the day any story that requires a  parent figure to fix the family unit  tends to fit neatly in that life finds a  way theme of the franchise following  Kingdom just involves that plot device  to own and clears relationship with blue  who becomes the endangered child of this  story and that rescue mission on its own  is enough in my opinion  but here's where we get to the problems  of the movie which there are plenty of  they're not that hard to find you can  read about them anywhere but I think the  core issue is the movie as a whole isn't  satisfied with its own dinosaur rescue  premise halfway through the story  departs Isla Nublar moving on to a  pretty far-fetched turn of events that I  won't spoil directly but I'll just say  it's kind of that stuff that Vincent  d'Onofrio's character hinted at in the  first movie now look I get why the movie  does this the turn sets up by onna to  deliver some truly spooky Dino in the  shadow imagery with the beasts entering  the world of man echoing the finale of  the lost world cool and it seemed like  the script wanted to go deeper into that  capitalist commentary from Jurassic  world to apply the if this then what  else logic of dinosaur technology to the  real world and try to evolve the  conversation  one could argue fallen Kingdom is trying  to bite the hand that beats it using the  greedy industrialist trying to weaponize  a pure piece of nature in the movie as a  metaphor for the greedy Hollywood studio  heads or we're saturated in a simple joy  that should have stayed the past I love  it when movies deconstruction honor  conventions like when Logan or Deadpool  defied the tropes of superhero movies  but these Jurassic world movies aren't  making those kind of creative sacrifices  to be truly deconstructive there's still  big spectacle based dinosaur movies like  their world hasn't really learned  anything from past disasters it just  keeps making the same mistakes  because let's face it those mistakes are  still a lot of fun to see unfold sadly  it just makes these attempts to evolve  the conversation feel really forced but  that's just my take I'm curious to know  your thoughts do you think falling  Kingdom would have been a better movie  if it ended halfway through comment down  below with your thoughts or tweet me  directly idea Voss and like this video  share it around subscribed into  rockstars for this kind of deeper  analysis and all the movies you like  check out our reviews and breakdowns of  everything Marvel and Star Wars Pixar DC  Game of Thrones Westworld thank you for  watching and as much as we hate on  digital editing technology one benefit  of chroma key is that our editors can  put me anywhere like this I'm a dinosaur  [Music]

